---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

// S4.3: honest placeholder privacy policy — accurate to what the site actually
// does, marked pending legal review. Set LAST_UPDATED to the cutover date at
// launch (S4.4 runbook).
const LAST_UPDATED = '7 August 2026';
---

<Base
  title="Tunnex — Privacy"
  description="What Tunnex collects, what it doesn't, and how to reach us — a plain-language privacy policy."
>
  <section class="mx-auto flex w-full max-w-2xl flex-col gap-6 px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 md:py-20">
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-3">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight">Privacy</h1>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-muted">Last updated: {LAST_UPDATED}</p>
    </div>

    <div
      class="rounded-md border border-l-4 border-warning/40 border-l-warning bg-warning/10 px-4 py-3 text-sm text-text"
      role="note"
    >
      <strong>Pre-launch document, pending legal review.</strong> This policy describes what we actually
      do today, in plain language. It will be replaced with a lawyer-reviewed version before any paid
      transaction. It is accurate, not boilerplate.
    </div>

    <div class="legal flex flex-col gap-6">
      <p>
        Tunnex builds a self-hosted VPN. The most important privacy fact is architectural: your
        network traffic, keys, configs, users, and logs never touch our systems — they stay on the
        infrastructure you run. This policy is only about the small amount of data the{' '}
        <strong>tunnex.io website</strong> handles.
      </p>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <h2>What stays entirely on your infrastructure</h2>
        <p>
          Worth stating plainly, because it is unusual and it is the reason this policy is short.
          Your Tunnex deployment <strong>never contacts us to function</strong> — licences are verified
          offline, so it works air-gapped. We therefore hold <strong>none</strong> of the following, and
          could not produce it if asked:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Your VPN traffic, WireGuard keys, device configurations, and address pools.</li>
          <li>Your users and their accounts, roles, groups, and 2FA enrolments.</li>
          <li>Your audit logs, access events, and policy rules.</li>
          <li>Which gateways or devices you run, where they are, or whether they are online.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          All of it lives in the deployment you operate. What we hold is described below, and it is
          the sales side of the relationship only.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <h2>What we hold, and where</h2>
        <p>
          Three tables in a Cloudflare D1 database attached to this website. Named, because "we
          collect some information" is not something you can check.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Trial and licence requests</strong> — your work email address and the company domain
            derived from it, plus, for a paid request, your company name and anything you typed in the
            notes field. Used to answer you and to decide the request.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Newsletter subscriptions</strong> — your email address, and nothing else.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The licence ledger</strong> — for every key we have issued: the company domain, the
            band, the term, and the key itself. It contains no personal contact details.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          <strong>Your IP address, transiently</strong>, to rate-limit form submissions and run bot
          protection (Cloudflare Turnstile). It is not stored as part of a profile.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <h2>What we don't do</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>No tracking cookies. No third-party trackers. No advertising networks.</li>
          <li>We never sell or share your data.</li>
          <li>
            Our analytics is <strong>Cloudflare Web Analytics</strong> — cookieless, with no cross-site
            tracking. That's why you won't see a cookie-consent banner: there are no cookies to consent
            to.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <h2>Email</h2>
        <p>
          Email we send is transactional — a verification link, a trial or license notice, or launch
          news you asked for. The newsletter is double opt-in: you confirm before we add you, and
          every message has an unsubscribe link.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>A request that produced no licence is deleted after 90 days</strong>,
            automatically. If you asked about Tunnex and nothing came of it, your email address does
            not sit here indefinitely.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>A request that produced a licence is kept</strong>, linked to that licence, for
            as long as the licence record exists.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The licence ledger is permanent.</strong> It is the only record of what we have issued
            — because deployments verify offline and never report back, it is the only way to answer whether
            a given key came from us. Deleting a row would not stop a key working; it would only stop
            us being able to say whose it is.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          To see what we hold, or to ask us to delete it, email{' '}
          <a href="mailto:support@tunnex.io">support@tunnex.io</a> and a human will handle it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <h2>Contact</h2>
        <p>
          Questions about privacy? <a href="mailto:support@tunnex.io">support@tunnex.io</a>.
          Security reports go to <a href="mailto:security@tunnex.io">security@tunnex.io</a>.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .legal {
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--t-text);
  }
  .legal :global(h2) {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }
  .legal :global(a) {
    color: var(--t-link);
    font-weight: 500;
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }
  .legal :global(a:hover) {
    color: var(--t-link-hover);
  }
  .legal :global(ul) {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    padding-left: 1.4rem;
    list-style: disc;
  }
  .legal :global(p) {
    color: var(--t-text-muted);
  }
  .legal :global(li) {
    color: var(--t-text-muted);
  }
  .legal :global(strong) {
    color: var(--t-text);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
